==================================================
GIT + GITHUB — STEP-BY-STEP CHEAT SHEET
Mac + VS Code
==================================================


PART 1 — FIRST TIME FOR A NEW PROJECT
======================================

STEP 1 — Open project in VS Code
---------------------------------
Open the main project folder in VS Code.

Example:
Python/


STEP 2 — Open VS Code Terminal
------------------------------
VS Code:
Terminal → New Terminal

Check current location:
pwd

Check files:
ls

Make sure you are inside your project folder.

Example:
.../Python


STEP 3 — Check Git
------------------
git --version

If Git is installed, you will see:
git version ...


STEP 4 — Initialize Git
-----------------------
Run ONLY ONCE for this project:

git init

This creates:
.git/

DO NOT run "git init" again inside
subfolders such as Pandas/ or Mini_project/.


STEP 5 — Create .gitignore
--------------------------
Create a file named:

.gitignore

Put this inside:

asi-venv/
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.vscode/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

IMPORTANT:
.gitignore must be a FILE, not a folder.


STEP 6 — Create GitHub repository
---------------------------------
Go to GitHub.

Create a new repository.

Example:
python-learning

For the easiest first setup:
Do NOT add:
- README
- .gitignore
- License

Keep the GitHub repository empty.


STEP 7 — Connect project to GitHub
----------------------------------
Copy the repository URL from GitHub.

Example:

git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/REPO.git

Check connection:

git remote -v

You should see:
origin ... (fetch)
origin ... (push)


STEP 8 — Set main branch
------------------------
git branch -M main


STEP 9 — Check files
--------------------
git status

Read this before adding files.

Make sure files such as:
asi-venv/
.DS_Store

are NOT going to be uploaded.


STEP 10 — Add files
-------------------
git add .


STEP 11 — Check staged files
----------------------------
git status

Check that only the files you actually
want on GitHub are listed.


STEP 12 — Create first commit
-----------------------------
git commit -m "Initial commit"


STEP 13 — Push to GitHub
------------------------
First push:

git push -u origin main

After successful push:
GitHub now contains your project.


==================================================
PART 2 — EVERY TIME YOU MAKE CHANGES
==================================================

Example:
You edit a Pandas notebook.

STEP 1 — Check what changed
---------------------------
git status


STEP 2 — Add changes
--------------------
git add .


STEP 3 — Check what will be committed
-------------------------------------
git status


STEP 4 — Commit
---------------
git commit -m "Add pandas practice"


STEP 5 — Push to GitHub
-----------------------
git push


