==================================================
WHAT EACH COMMAND MEANS
==================================================

git init
= Start Git tracking for a project.

git status
= Show what changed.

git add .
= Prepare all changes for commit.

git commit -m "message"
= Save a version/checkpoint locally.

git push
= Upload committed changes to GitHub.

git remote -v
= Check which GitHub repository is connected.

git branch
= Show current branches.

git log --oneline
= Show previous commits.

git reset
= Unstage changes without deleting files.


==================================================
IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE
==================================================

git add
= Prepare changes

git commit
= Save changes LOCALLY

git push
= Upload changes to GITHUB


==================================================
COMMON PROBLEMS
==================================================

1. "remote origin already exists"

Don't run:
git remote add origin ...

again.

Check:
git remote -v

If the URL is correct, continue.


2. "nothing to commit, working tree clean"

This means:
There are no new changes to commit.

If you expected changes, run:
git status


3. "git add ." adds asi-venv

Your .gitignore is wrong/missing.

Check:
cat .gitignore

Make sure it contains:
asi-venv/


4. .DS_Store appears in git status

Add this to .gitignore:
.DS_Store

Then:
git add .


5. A subfolder has its own .git

Example:
Mini_project/.git

This means that subfolder is a separate
Git repository.

For a normal single-repository project,
remove ONLY the nested Git metadata:

rm -rf Mini_project/.git

WARNING:
Do NOT delete the actual project folder.


6. Need to remove staged files

git reset

This UNSTAGES files.
It does NOT delete them.


